# Tor instance dedicated to the reputation-gated egress PoC.
# Runs on its own ports so it never collides with any other tor on this machine
# (e.g. the searxng-tor-bench container on 9050/9051).

SocksPort 9250
ControlPort 9251
CookieAuthentication 1

# Keep all state inside the project so the PoC is self-contained and the onion
# address is stable across restarts (the key lives under hs/).
DataDirectory ./tor/data

# Publish the gateway as a v3 onion service. The gateway process listens on
# 127.0.0.1:8443; Tor exposes it at <addr>.onion:80. There is NO exit node in
# the client -> gateway path: the connection is rendezvous-routed and stays
# inside Tor, so the client IP is never revealed to the gateway and the gateway
# is not subject to Tor-exit IP reputation.
HiddenServiceDir ./tor/hs
HiddenServicePort 80 127.0.0.1:8443

# Anti-DoS tier: Tor's built-in onion-service proof-of-work defense makes flooding
# the rendezvous (before any zk verification work) costly. It is the cheap outer
# gate; the reputation proof is the expensive inner gate.
#
# We do NOT set HiddenServicePoWDefensesEnabled here, on purpose: stock tor hard-
# fails config validation if the option is set without the 'pow' module, and the
# Homebrew bottle ships without it (the PoW code is GPL; the bottle is BSD-only).
# So start-tor.sh enables the defense at runtime via --HiddenServicePoWDefensesEnabled
# only when it detects a tor that has the module (see scripts/build-tor-pow.sh).
# That keeps this PoC running unchanged on 100% stock tor, with PoW as an
# opportunistic upgrade rather than a hard requirement.

Log notice stdout
